"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[7201],{27201:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"slug":"benzin-podorozhanie-2-1-rublya-import-oktyabr-2026","locale":"en","title":"Energy Ministry proposes a 2.1-ruble-per-litre rise in petrol and diesel prices","description":"The Energy Ministry proposes allowing oil companies to raise petrol and diesel prices by 2.1 rubles per litre to cover fuel-import losses.","blocks":[{"id":"b001","kind":"paragraph","text":"In October, Deputy Prime Minister Alexander Novak forwarded the Energy Ministry\'s fuel-pricing proposal to Prime Minister Mikhail Mishustin. It says imported petrol and diesel sold at vertically integrated oil companies\' filling stations lose 13–18 rubles per litre, even after the import compensation payment. The ministry proposed two solutions. First, raise prices at those stations by 2.1 rubles per litre. Second, raise them by 1.6 rubles and include rail transport from northwestern Russia to the country\'s central regions in the compensation mechanism."},{"id":"b002","kind":"paragraph","text":"Imports became necessary because domestic petrol is insufficient; Novak estimates the shortage at roughly 5%. Russia imports fuel from Belarus and by sea, including from India. From October to December, petrol imports may reach around 620 thousand tonnes monthly, or 16–18% of domestic supply. Novak himself cited a share around 10%. Imports will continue at least until year-end."},{"id":"b003","kind":"paragraph","text":"Imagine a bakery short of its own flour. It buys the missing bags at a shop for twice the price but sells bread at the old price. Each loaf made from purchased flour loses money. Someone must cover it: the bakery, the city through a subsidy, or the customer through dearer bread. In fuel markets, that purchased-bag cost is called import parity. In September, imported AI-92 petrol cost 108 thousand rubles per tonne, against 70.17 thousand for Russian petrol on the exchange. Converted to litres, the gap was around 28 rubles."},{"id":"b004","kind":"paragraph","text":"The import compensation payment covers part of the gap from the budget. But it excludes sellers\' sanctions-risk markup and transport within Russia. The Finance Ministry opposes expanding it. Market participants estimate that a 2.1-ruble increase would bring refiners around 12 billion rubles of extra monthly revenue without budget expenditure. The Energy Ministry estimates that, with this increase, petrol at oil-company stations would be up 11% by year-end, or 7.1 rubles per litre; summer diesel would be up 10.7%, or 8.2 rubles. These figures concern oil-company stations specifically: their prices rose more slowly than independents\' during summer."},{"id":"b005","kind":"paragraph","text":"Across all stations, average petrol cost 78.04 rubles per litre on October 19, up 20.53% since year-start against overall inflation of 6.15%. Diesel cost 86.58 rubles, up 13.38%. Petrol fell 0.69% over the latest week. Queues disappeared in most regions, seasonal demand weakened and independent stations, which raised prices fastest in summer, began cutting them."},{"id":"b006","kind":"paragraph","text":"The government has not decided yet. An increase at oil-company stations would narrow their losses but lift the price floor. If oil companies also raise wholesale prices, independent stations buying their fuel would have to raise prices too. Cheaper oil and imports would narrow the gap requiring compensation. Further refinery attacks would increase the import share and the loss on each litre."},{"id":"b007","kind":"heading","text":"Will petrol rise by 2.1 rubles per litre?"},{"id":"b008","kind":"paragraph","text":"This is an Energy Ministry proposal, not yet a government decision. It concerns stations owned by vertically integrated oil companies. The ministry estimates that with the increase, their petrol would be up 11%, or 7.1 rubles per litre, by year-end, and summer diesel up 10.7%, or 8.2 rubles."},{"id":"b009","kind":"heading","text":"Why do oil companies lose money on imported petrol?"},{"id":"b010","kind":"paragraph","text":"Domestic petrol is insufficient, so some must be imported. In September, imported AI-92 cost 108 thousand rubles per tonne, versus 70.17 thousand for domestic exchange-traded petrol. The mechanism covers part of the difference, but not sanctions-risk markups or Russian transport. Each imported litre consequently loses 13–18 rubles."},{"id":"b011","kind":"heading","text":"Which option is cheaper for drivers?"},{"id":"b012","kind":"paragraph","text":"The second: include rail delivery from the northwest to central Russia in the compensation mechanism. Prices would rise by 1.6 rubles per litre, but the budget would pay oil companies up to 2 billion rubles monthly. The Finance Ministry does not support this option."}],"sources":[{"label":"The cost of encouraging petrol imports is being shifted to consumers","url":"https://www.kommersant.ru/doc/9045149"},{"label":"Oil companies may raise filling-station prices by 2.1 rubles","url":"https://www.kommersant.ru/doc/9045179"},{"label":"Fuel supplied from abroad","url":"https://kommersant.ru/doc/9027583"},{"label":"Russian petrol prices fell by 0.69%","url":"https://www.interfax.ru/business/1124061"},{"label":"Demand is no longer putting pressure on prices","url":"https://rg.ru/2026/10/22/spros-ne-davit.html"}],"sourceBodySha256":"9e2e55674eb7e77b6baeb4a6291922463595de9952c285600f9debb06446d035"}')}}]);